<Layout
	title="About Me"
	description="Horologiarius: essays about cosmology, philosophy and the underlying architecture of things."
	pubDate={new Date('September 01 2026')}
	heroImage={AboutHeroImage}
>
	<p>
		Philosophy and linguistics enthusiast interested in the mechanisms beneath things.
	</p>

	<p>
		*Horologiarius* means Watchmaker and it is a personal workshop for philosophy, science, language, culture,
        literature, and the questions that emerge when one subject refuses to remain confined to itself.
	</p>

	<p>
		I write mostly from curiosity: reading leads to a question, a question to a distinction, a distinction
        to another question. Some notes become essays; others quotes from great minds; and the rest remains
        fragments, marginalia, or unfinished attempts to understand something that still resists a conclusion.
	</p>

	<p>
		I am less interested in collecting answers than in examining how answers are built.
	</p>

	<p>
		Here you will find reflections on books, ideas, scientific concepts, language, psychology,
        mathematics, metaphysics, music, physics, theory of mind, culture, and occasionally the strange
        intersections between them. Some texts are deliberate; others are exploratory. None of them claim to
        be the final word.
	</p>

    <p>
        The name is a metaphor as much as an identity.
    </p>

    <p>
        A watchmaker does not merely look at a clock. He opens it, studies the relations between its parts,
        traces the movement from one gear to another, and asks what makes the whole mechanism possible.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is what I try to do with ideas.
    </p>

    <p>
        — *The Watchmaker*
    </p>

</Layout>
